\newcommand{\cde}[1]{{\small\texttt{#1}}}

\newpage

\section{Low-level MABC interface}

SelectionTools provides two interfaces for marker-assisted
backcrossing (MABC).  The low-level interface uses the 
population-simulation functions of SelectionTools and therefore
exposes every crossing, selection and sampling step.  The high-level
interface, implemented by the \cde{mab.*} functions, describes a
complete backcross program by vectors of generation types, population
sizes and selection strategies.  Both interfaces use the same
simulation engine.

The examples in this vignette are based on the MABC studies of
Frisch, Bohn and Melchinger (1999) and Frisch and Melchinger (2001).
They illustrate recovery of the recurrent-parent genome, linkage drag,
marker-data requirements, simultaneous introgression of several target
genes, and the use of observed parental or backcross marker genotypes.


The low-level interface is appropriate when individual operations of a
breeding program must be controlled explicitly.  
At this level, foreground selection, flanking-marker selection and
genome-wide background selection are separate commands.  This makes the
interface useful for developing new breeding schemes or for studying exactly
which individuals enter each selection stage.

The low-level examples use the package data sets \cde{lib00.map1},
\cde{lib00.map1a}, and \cde{lib00.map2}.  They reproduce the structure of
the simulations underlying Tables 1--3 of Frisch et al.\ (1999).  In the
low-level interface the donor allele is coded as 1 and the recurrent-parent
allele as 8.

\paragraph{Simulation size and comparison with published results.}
The simulation studies of Frisch, Bohn and Melchinger (1999) and Frisch
and Melchinger (2001) used 10000 replicate breeding programs.  The examples
use \cde{NREP <- 10000} for the same sampling precision; the unselected
baseline also contains 10000 individuals per generation.  Repetitions are
distinct from the number of plants per breeding generation.  The latter
controls selection intensity and is specified separately by
\cde{population.size} or the population-size argument of \cde{cross()}.
Increasing repetitions reduces sampling variation without changing the
breeding scheme.  Comparable published tables also require the same map,
selection strategy and generations; identical numerical entries are not
expected from independently seeded simulations.

\paragraph{Simulation output and random-number state.}
SelectionTools uses clock-based initialization by default.  For reproducible
simulations, \cde{rng.init(seed=1001)} initializes R's random-number state
and uses R's generator to supply seeds for the internal simulation generators.
A call to \cde{set.seed()} alone does not initialize those internal generators.
Calling \cde{rng.init()} without a seed returns to clock-based initialization.

The examples select one thread and initialize the generators before simulating.
Repeating the same seed, inputs and sequence of calls gives identical results
with the same actual thread count, R random-number generator settings, package
build and numerical libraries.  Results need not agree across different thread
counts or platforms.  The blue boxes contain output from these seeded runs.

\subsection{Baseline backcrossing and marker density}

Without selection, repeated backcrossing to the recurrent parent provides
the baseline increase in RPG.  The original example also compared a map with
approximately 80 markers with a reduced map containing approximately 40
markers.  Marker effects are scaled so that the marker score estimates the
recurrent-parent genome proportion.

% MABC example: low_baseline
<<low_baseline_01,eval=FALSE>>=
library(SelectionTools)
options(width=72, digits=5)
st.set.num.threads(1)

st.output.dir <- file.path(tempdir(), "SelectionTools-masbc")
st.data.dir <- st.output.dir
dir.create(st.output.dir, recursive=TRUE, showWarnings=FALSE)

NREP <- 10000

rng.init(seed=1001)
reset.all()
@
The call to \cde{rng.init(seed=1001)} initializes both R and the internal random-number generators, and \cde{reset.all()}
removes state left by previous low-level simulations.  The repeated setup
is intentional so this example can be run independently.

<<low_baseline_02,eval=FALSE>>=
data("lib00.map1")
data("lib00.map1a")
write.table(lib00.map1, file=file.path(st.output.dir,"mab-1.map"),
            quote=FALSE, row.names=FALSE, col.names=FALSE)
write.table(lib00.map1a, file=file.path(st.output.dir,"mab-1a.map"),
            quote=FALSE, row.names=FALSE, col.names=FALSE)

set.genome.par(
    no.chrom=10,
    no.hom=2,
    chrom.len=c(2.68,1.81,2.08,1.40,1.53,
                1.56,0.93,1.44,1.55,1.14)
)
load.linkage.map(file.path(st.output.dir,"mab-1.map"))
@
The package maps are written to temporary files because the low-level
linkage-map loader expects a file.  The genome definition supplies the ten
maize chromosome lengths used in the original MABC examples before the
denser map is loaded.

<<low_baseline_03,eval=FALSE>>=
init.population("P1",homozygote(1))
init.population("P2",homozygote(8))
cross("F1","P1","P2",NREP)

gen <- c("P1","F1","BC1","BC2","BC3","BC4","BC5","BC6")
for (i in 1:7)
    cross(gen[i+1],gen[i],"P2",NREP)

rpg <- genome.contribution("BC1 BC2 BC3 BC4 BC5 BC6",8)

define.effects("markers80","0, mc 8 uniform 0.00625")
estimated80 <- get.score("BC2","markers80")
true80 <- evaluate.genome("BC2",8)$ratio
@
The donor and recurrent parents are homozygous for alleles 1 and 8,
respectively.  Six successive backcrosses provide the no-selection
baseline, after which the true recurrent-parent genome proportion and its
marker-based estimate are obtained for the denser map.

<<low_baseline_04,eval=FALSE>>=
load.linkage.map(file.path(st.output.dir,"mab-1a.map"))
define.effects("markers40","0, mc 8 uniform 0.0125")
estimated40 <- get.score("BC2","markers40")
true40 <- evaluate.genome("BC2",8)$ratio

out <- list(
    recurrent.parent.genome=round(rpg,4),
    marker.estimate.correlation=c(
        markers80=cor(estimated80,true80),
        markers40=cor(estimated40,true40)
    )
)
print(out)
@
The reduced map is then loaded and the same marker estimate is formed with
appropriately rescaled marker effects.  The reported correlations show how
marker density affects the approximation of the true RPG.

\begin{Soutput}
$recurrent.parent.genome
      Obs   Mean   SDev    Min    Q10    Med    Max
BC1 10000 0.7489 0.0525 0.5693 0.6806 0.7490 0.9352
BC2 10000 0.8737 0.0423 0.7051 0.8173 0.8755 0.9993
BC3 10000 0.9373 0.0301 0.8023 0.8966 0.9408 1.0000
BC4 10000 0.9685 0.0204 0.8641 0.9411 0.9713 1.0000
BC5 10000 0.9841 0.0136 0.8991 0.9659 0.9871 1.0000
BC6 10000 0.9921 0.0091 0.9355 0.9797 0.9949 1.0000

$marker.estimate.correlation
markers80 markers40 
  0.90560   0.86971 

\end{Soutput}


The point of the marker-density comparison is not to define a new selection
strategy, but to show how densely distributed background markers approximate
the true RPG recovered from the simulated genome.

\subsection{Foreground selection for the target allele}

Foreground selection retains plants carrying the target allele.  At the
low level, each backcross is generated explicitly, all best plants for the
target effect are retained, one plant is sampled, and the selected plant is
used as the non-recurrent parent of the next backcross.

% MABC example: low_foreground
<<low_foreground_01,eval=FALSE>>=
library(SelectionTools)
options(width=72, digits=5)
st.set.num.threads(1)

st.output.dir <- file.path(tempdir(), "SelectionTools-masbc")
st.data.dir <- st.output.dir
dir.create(st.output.dir, recursive=TRUE, showWarnings=FALSE)

NREP <- 10000

rng.init(seed=1002)
reset.all()
@
This independent run again fixes the simulation environment and
random-number seed before any populations are created.

<<low_foreground_02,eval=FALSE>>=
data("lib00.map1")
write.table(lib00.map1, file=file.path(st.output.dir,"mab-1.map"),
            quote=FALSE, row.names=FALSE, col.names=FALSE)

set.genome.par(
    no.chrom=10,
    no.hom=2,
    chrom.len=c(2.68,1.81,2.08,1.40,1.53,
                1.56,0.93,1.44,1.55,1.14)
)
load.linkage.map(file.path(st.output.dir,"mab-1.map"))
define.effects("target","0, target 1 uniform 1")
@
The historical map is materialized and loaded, and a target effect is
defined so that allele 1 at the target locus identifies plants carrying the
donor target allele.

<<low_foreground_03,eval=FALSE>>=
init.population("P1",homozygote(1))
init.population("P2",homozygote(8))
cross("F1","P1","P2",1)

pop <- c("F1","BC1","BC2","BC3","BC4","BC5","BC6")
se1 <- paste(pop,"s1",sep="")
sel <- paste(pop,"se",sep="")
store <- paste(pop,"st",sep="")
sel[1] <- "F1"
@
The two homozygous parents generate the F1.  Population-name vectors are
prepared for the six backcross generations and for the intermediate and
stored selected populations.

<<low_foreground_04,eval=FALSE>>=
for (j in 1:NREP) {
    for (i in 1:6) {
        cross(pop[i+1],sel[i],"P2",50)
        select.all.best(se1[i+1],pop[i+1],"target")
        sample.population(sel[i+1],se1[i+1],1)
        append.population(store[i+1],sel[i+1])
    }
}
@
For each replicate and generation, a backcross population of 50 plants is
produced.  All plants with the best target score are retained, one is
sampled, and that plant becomes the non-recurrent parent of the next
backcross.

<<low_foreground_05,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- round(
    genome.contribution(
        "BC1st BC2st BC3st BC4st BC5st BC6st",8
    ),
    4
)
print(out)
@
The stored selected plants are finally summarized by their recurrent-parent
genome contribution.  This is the low-level implementation of pure
foreground selection.

\begin{Soutput}
        Obs   Mean   SDev    Min    Q10    Med    Max
BC1st 10000 0.7397 0.0515 0.5597 0.6731 0.7402 0.9304
BC2st 10000 0.8608 0.0423 0.6852 0.8054 0.8629 0.9796
BC3st 10000 0.9235 0.0303 0.7709 0.8828 0.9260 0.9952
BC4st 10000 0.9556 0.0215 0.8436 0.9261 0.9586 0.9994
BC5st 10000 0.9725 0.0153 0.8860 0.9518 0.9751 0.9995
BC6st 10000 0.9817 0.0111 0.9032 0.9668 0.9839 0.9999
\end{Soutput}


\subsection{Two-stage selection}

Two-stage selection first retains target carriers and then selects among them
for the largest recurrent-parent marker score.  The low-level interface also
allows the number of marker data points to be accumulated with
\cde{evaluate.mdp()} and \cde{get.mdp()}.

% MABC example: low_two_stage
<<low_two_stage_01,eval=FALSE>>=
library(SelectionTools)
options(width=72, digits=5)
st.set.num.threads(1)

st.output.dir <- file.path(tempdir(), "SelectionTools-masbc")
st.data.dir <- st.output.dir
dir.create(st.output.dir, recursive=TRUE, showWarnings=FALSE)

NREP <- 10000

rng.init(seed=1003)
reset.all()
@
The two-stage example is again independent and runnable.  Resetting the
simulation state is especially important because marker-data-point counters
are used below.

<<low_two_stage_02,eval=FALSE>>=
data("lib00.map1")
write.table(lib00.map1, file=file.path(st.output.dir,"mab-1.map"),
            quote=FALSE, row.names=FALSE, col.names=FALSE)

set.genome.par(
    no.chrom=10,
    no.hom=2,
    chrom.len=c(2.68,1.81,2.08,1.40,1.53,
                1.56,0.93,1.44,1.55,1.14)
)
load.linkage.map(file.path(st.output.dir,"mab-1.map"))
define.effects("target","0, target 1 uniform 1")
define.effects("markers","0, mc 8 uniform 0.00625")
@
The target effect identifies target carriers, whereas the genome-wide
marker effect rewards recurrent-parent allele 8 across the map.  This
separates foreground and background selection explicitly.

<<low_two_stage_03,eval=FALSE>>=
init.population("P1",homozygote(1))
init.population("P2",homozygote(8))
cross("F1","P1","P2",1)

pop <- c("F1","BC1","BC2","BC3","BC4","BC5","BC6",
         "BC7","BC8","BC9","BC10")
se1 <- paste(pop,"s1",sep="")
se2 <- paste(pop,"s2",sep="")
sel <- paste(pop,"se",sep="")
store <- paste(pop,"st",sep="")
sel[1] <- "F1"

mdp <- matrix(NA_real_,nrow=10,ncol=NREP)
@
Parents and F1 are created and names for ten backcross generations are
prepared.  The \cde{mdp} matrix stores cumulative marker data points
separately for every generation and replicate.

<<low_two_stage_04,eval=FALSE>>=
for (j in 1:NREP) {
    reset.mdp()
    for (i in 1:10) {
        cross(pop[i+1],sel[i],"P2",100)
        select.all.best(se1[i+1],pop[i+1],"target")
        evaluate.mdp(se1[i+1],sel[i],"P2","markers")
        mdp[i,j] <- get.mdp()
        select.all.best(se2[i+1],se1[i+1],"markers")
        sample.population(sel[i+1],se2[i+1],1)
        append.population(store[i+1],sel[i+1])
    }
}
@
Within each backcross generation, target carriers are selected first.
Marker requirements are then counted for these candidates, background
selection chooses the best recurrent-parent marker score, and one plant is
sampled for the next generation.

<<low_two_stage_05,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- list(
    recurrent.parent.genome=round(
        genome.contribution(
            paste0("BC",1:10,"st",collapse=" "),8
        ),
        4
    ),
    mean.marker.data.points=data.frame(
        generation=paste0("BC",1:10),
        MDP=round(rowMeans(mdp),1)
    )
)
print(out)
@
The result combines the realized RPG of the selected plants with the
average cumulative marker-data requirement.  Thus biological response and
genotyping effort can be compared in the same example.

\begin{Soutput}
$recurrent.parent.genome
         Obs   Mean   SDev    Min    Q10    Med    Max
BC1st  10000 0.8438 0.0281 0.7363 0.8081 0.8432 0.9402
BC2st  10000 0.9573 0.0161 0.8837 0.9358 0.9585 0.9968
BC3st  10000 0.9860 0.0084 0.9366 0.9743 0.9874 0.9998
BC4st  10000 0.9928 0.0054 0.9567 0.9857 0.9943 0.9999
BC5st  10000 0.9948 0.0038 0.9638 0.9900 0.9958 0.9999
BC6st  10000 0.9959 0.0029 0.9728 0.9928 0.9965 0.9999
BC7st  10000 0.9965 0.0023 0.9765 0.9940 0.9969 0.9999
BC8st  10000 0.9968 0.0019 0.9812 0.9945 0.9972 0.9999
BC9st  10000 0.9971 0.0017 0.9812 0.9949 0.9973 0.9999
BC10st 10000 0.9973 0.0016 0.9860 0.9951 0.9975 1.0000

$mean.marker.data.points
   generation    MDP
1         BC1 4003.7
2         BC2 5179.9
3         BC3 5437.1
4         BC4 5475.2
5         BC5 5477.3
6         BC6 5477.4
7         BC7 5477.4
8         BC8 5477.4
9         BC9 5477.4
10       BC10 5477.4

\end{Soutput}


\subsection{Three-stage selection}

The three-stage strategy first selects for the target allele, then for
recurrent-parent alleles at flanking markers, and finally for the
genome-wide recurrent-parent marker score.  The flanking step enriches for
recombinants close to the target and thereby reduces linkage drag before
the genome-wide background-selection step.

% MABC example: low_three_stage
<<low_three_stage_01,eval=FALSE>>=
library(SelectionTools)
options(width=72, digits=5)
st.set.num.threads(1)

st.output.dir <- file.path(tempdir(), "SelectionTools-masbc")
st.data.dir <- st.output.dir
dir.create(st.output.dir, recursive=TRUE, showWarnings=FALSE)

NREP <- 10000

rng.init(seed=1004)
reset.all()
@
The three-stage simulation uses its own seed and clean simulation state,
exactly as the preceding low-level examples.

<<low_three_stage_02,eval=FALSE>>=
data("lib00.map2")
write.table(lib00.map2, file=file.path(st.output.dir,"mab-2.map"),
            quote=FALSE, row.names=FALSE, col.names=FALSE)

set.genome.par(
    no.chrom=10,
    no.hom=2,
    chrom.len=c(2.68,1.81,2.08,1.40,1.53,
                1.56,0.93,1.44,1.55,1.14)
)
load.linkage.map(file.path(st.output.dir,"mab-2.map"))
define.effects("target","0, target 1 uniform 1")
define.effects("flanking","0, flanking 8 uniform 1")
define.effects("markers","0, mc 8 uniform 1")
@
The second historical map contains the target and its flanking loci.
Separate effects are defined for the target, the flanking markers, and the
genome-wide recurrent-parent marker score.

<<low_three_stage_03,eval=FALSE>>=
init.population("P1",homozygote(1))
init.population("P2",homozygote(8))
cross("F1","P1","P2",1)

pop <- c("F1","BC1","BC2","BC3","BC4","BC5","BC6")
s1 <- paste(pop,"s1",sep="")
s2 <- paste(pop,"s2",sep="")
sel <- paste(pop,"se",sep="")
store <- paste(pop,"st",sep="")
sel[1] <- "F1"

mdp <- matrix(NA_real_,nrow=6,ncol=NREP)
@
Parents and F1 are initialized and population names are prepared for six
backcross generations.  A marker-data-point matrix again records genotyping
effort over replicates.

<<low_three_stage_04,eval=FALSE>>=
for (j in 1:NREP) {
    reset.mdp()
    for (i in 1:6) {
        cross(pop[i+1],sel[i],"P2",100)
        select.all.best(s1[i+1],pop[i+1],"target")
        evaluate.mdp(s1[i+1],sel[i],"P2","flanking")
        select.all.best(s2[i+1],s1[i+1],"flanking")
        evaluate.mdp(s2[i+1],sel[i],"P2","markers")
        select.n.best(sel[i+1],s2[i+1],"markers",1)
        append.population(store[i+1],sel[i+1])
        mdp[i,j] <- get.mdp()
    }
}
@
Each generation performs the three selection stages in order: target
carriers, recurrent-parent alleles at flanking markers, and finally
genome-wide background selection.  The selected plant is stored and used in
the next backcross.

<<low_three_stage_05,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- list(
    recurrent.parent.genome=round(
        genome.contribution(
            "BC1st BC2st BC3st BC4st BC5st BC6st",8
        ),
        4
    ),
    mean.marker.data.points=data.frame(
        generation=paste0("BC",1:6),
        MDP=round(rowMeans(mdp),1)
    )
)
print(out)
@
The final summary reports RPG together with marker-data requirements.
Relative to two-stage selection, the additional flanking step is intended
to reduce donor chromosome surrounding the target.

\begin{Soutput}
$recurrent.parent.genome
        Obs   Mean   SDev    Min    Q10    Med    Max
BC1st 10000 0.7993 0.0487 0.6004 0.7331 0.8049 0.9512
BC2st 10000 0.9350 0.0265 0.8050 0.9005 0.9370 0.9979
BC3st 10000 0.9851 0.0101 0.9280 0.9712 0.9872 0.9999
BC4st 10000 0.9922 0.0059 0.9374 0.9842 0.9939 1.0000
BC5st 10000 0.9945 0.0042 0.9594 0.9891 0.9956 1.0000
BC6st 10000 0.9958 0.0032 0.9710 0.9921 0.9965 1.0000

$mean.marker.data.points
  generation    MDP
1        BC1  593.1
2        BC2 1373.6
3        BC3 1797.9
4        BC4 1826.0
5        BC5 1826.1
6        BC6 1826.1

\end{Soutput}


These examples illustrate why the low-level interface remains useful even
though the high-level interface is much shorter: every intermediate
population and every marker-analysis step can be inspected or modified.

\section{High-level MABC workflow}

The high-level interface comprises four principal functions.
\begin{description}
\item[\cde{mab.simulate()}] simulates one MABC program and saves the complete
result object in an explicitly supplied result file.
\item[\cde{mab.compare()}] runs up to nine related scenarios.  Scenario A is
the base definition; scenarios B through I inherit every setting from A
unless the corresponding scenario-specific argument is supplied.
\item[\cde{mab.load.data()}] loads one result object from an explicitly
supplied result file.
\item[\cde{mab.tabulate()}] tabulates one or more saved scenarios and returns
the requested summaries.
\end{description}
The high-level interface is convenient for comparing alternative allocations and breeding
strategies.  The low-level interface is more flexible when the sequence of
operations itself is the object of study.

\subsection{Input data and terminology}

\subsubsection{Input directories and result files}

The high-level MABC routines obtain linkage maps and parental marker data
from \cde{st.input.dir}.  Result files are written relative to
\cde{st.data.dir}; recoded parental marker files are written relative to
\cde{st.output.dir}.  Absolute file names can also be supplied.  The
high-level routines require explicit \cde{result.file} or
\cde{result.files} arguments; result-file names are not generated
automatically.

Each simulation example calls \cde{rng.init(seed=...)} after the shared
setup to initialize both R and the internal random-number generators.
Use the same seed, inputs, call sequence and actual thread count in the same
software environment to repeat a result.  Standard startup remains clock-based
when no seed is requested; \cde{rng.init()} restores that behavior.

The following shared setup prepares the data and directories for all
high-level examples. Run it once before selecting an example:

\begin{Sinput}
library(SelectionTools)
options(width=72, digits=5)
st.set.num.threads(1)

data("masbc.input")
st.input.dir <- file.path(tempdir(), "SelectionTools-masbc-input")
dir.create(st.input.dir, recursive=TRUE, showWarnings=FALSE)
for (file in names(masbc.input))
    writeLines(masbc.input[[file]], file.path(st.input.dir,file))
st.output.dir <- file.path(tempdir(), "SelectionTools-masbc")
st.data.dir <- st.output.dir
dir.create(st.output.dir, recursive=TRUE, showWarnings=FALSE)

NREP <- 10000
\end{Sinput}

The package data set \cde{masbc.input} contains the 16 text files used by
the high-level examples as a named list of character vectors.  It contains
\cde{ex-mab1.map} through \cde{ex-mab4.map}, \cde{ex-mabp1.mda},
\cde{ex-mabp2.mda}, \cde{ex-mabp1\_m.mda}, \cde{ex-mabp2\_m.mda}, and
\cde{ex-mabi1.mda} through \cde{ex-mabi8.mda}.  The \cde{mab.*}
functions use file names, so the shared setup loads \cde{masbc.input}
with \cde{data()} and writes its contents to a temporary input directory.  No permanent files are created in the user's working
directory.

\subsubsection{Linkage-map files}

A linkage-map file contains three fields: chromosome number, position in cM,
and locus name.  A short excerpt from \cde{ex-mab1.map} is:

\begin{Soutput}
1       0      umc94
1       70     umc137
5       23     umc90
5       30     target
5       46     umc27
5       57     umc43
\end{Soutput}

The first MABC examples assume homozygous donor and recurrent parents and
polymorphism between them at every marker on the map.  In this mode,
\cde{donor.parent="H"} and \cde{recurrent.parent="H"} are used.

\subsubsection{Parental marker files}

Observed parental marker genotypes can be supplied instead.  The first line
contains the genotype name.  For a homozygous parent, each following line
contains marker name and one allele, for example:

\begin{Soutput}
01E0103DH03
s1e2855xxx ade
s1e2083xxx ade
s1e4552xxx gua
s1e4867xxx cyt
\end{Soutput}

For a heterozygous parent, two alleles are given:

\begin{Soutput}
bc1i1
umc94   1  8
bnl805  1  8
umc76   8  8
umc137  8  8
\end{Soutput}

If only one allele is present in the file, the genotype is treated as
homozygous.  When parental files are used, donor and recurrent parent must
both be supplied as files.  Only markers that occur in the linkage map and
in both parental files and that are not coded as missing are retained.
The missing-allele symbol is controlled by \cde{missing.allele}; the default
is \cde{"9"}.

The following input-data example reads the files directly and verifies
the linkage-map and parental-marker file structure.

% MABC example: input_overview
<<input_overview_01,eval=FALSE>>=
# Input files have been prepared by the shared setup.
@
The shared setup loads the package, fixes output formatting, selects a
single thread, prepares the input directory, and directs results to a
temporary directory.  \cde{NREP} is fixed at 10000 throughout the vignette.

<<input_overview_02,eval=FALSE>>=
map <- read.table(
    file.path(st.input.dir,"ex-mab1.map"),
    stringsAsFactors=FALSE
)
names(map) <- c("chromosome","position.cM","locus")
@
The map file is ordinary white-space separated text.  Reading it directly
shows the three required fields: chromosome, position in cM, and locus
name.

<<input_overview_03,eval=FALSE>>=
parent <- read.table(
    file.path(st.input.dir,"ex-mabp1_m.mda"),
    stringsAsFactors=FALSE,
    skip=1
)
parent.name <- scan(
    file.path(st.input.dir,"ex-mabp1_m.mda"),
    what="character",
    n=1,
    quiet=TRUE
)
@
The parental marker file begins with the genotype name and is followed by
marker--allele records.  The first call reads the marker table, while
\cde{scan()} retrieves the name stored on the first line.

<<input_overview_04,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- list(
    map=head(map,6),
    parent.name=parent.name,
    parent.markers=head(parent,6)
)
print(out)
@
Only a short prefix is printed here.  This makes the file structure visible
without reproducing the complete map and parental genotype file in the
vignette.

\begin{Soutput}
$map
  chromosome position.cM  locus
1          1           0  umc94
2          1           2 bnl805
3          1          67  umc76
4          1          70 umc137
5          1         141  umc58
6          1         160  umc23

$parent.name
[1] "01E0103DH03"

$parent.markers
          V1  V2
1 s1e2855xxx ade
2 s1e2083xxx ade
3 s1e4552xxx gua
4 s1e4867xxx cyt
5 s1e3305xxx cyt
6 s1e2837xxx cyt

\end{Soutput}


\subsubsection{Generation types}

A high-level breeding program is described by parallel vectors.  The
implemented generation types are:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\cde{f1} & F1 population\\
\cde{bc} & backcross population\\
\cde{s}  & selfing population\\
\cde{dh} & doubled haploid population
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The vectors \cde{gen.type}, \cde{population.size},
\cde{sel.strategy}, and \cde{no.selected} must describe the same number of
generations.  If \cde{no.selected} is omitted, one individual is selected in
each generation.  \cde{no.preselected}, when supplied, must likewise have
one entry per generation.

\subsubsection{Selection strategies}

The high-level selection code is a sequence of letters describing the
selection stages.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{10.5cm}}
\cde{n} & random selection\\
\cde{t} & donor alleles at target loci\\
\cde{tb} & target selection followed by genome-wide background selection\\
\cde{tf} & target selection followed by recurrent-parent alleles at flanking loci\\
\cde{tfb} & target, flanking-marker, and genome-wide background selection\\
\cde{tfs} & target and flanking-marker selection followed by selection for a low number of donor chromosome segments\\
\cde{tr} & target selection followed by recurrent-parent alleles at recipient loci\\
\cde{trb} & target, recipient-locus, and genome-wide background selection\\
\cde{trfb} & target, recipient-locus, flanking-marker, and genome-wide background selection\\
\cde{tfrb} & target, flanking-marker, recipient-locus, and genome-wide background selection
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The two-stage strategy \cde{tb} and the three-stage strategy \cde{tfb}
correspond to the strategies described by Frisch et al.\ (1999).
\cde{flanking.loci} normally flank the target locus and are used to reduce
the donor chromosome segment attached to it, but they need not literally be
the nearest flanking markers.  \cde{recipient.loci} define recurrent-parent
alleles or chromosome regions that must be retained in the final
backcrossing product.

\subsubsection{Counting marker analyses}

The high-level simulator also records marker-data requirements.
The papers count individual marker data points (MDP).  An HT assay here
counts one genotyped individual, not each marker scored on that individual;
therefore HT totals must not be compared numerically with published MDP
totals.  The low-level examples explicitly accumulate MDP.

\begin{description}
\item[\cde{t}] Foreground selection for the target genes is not counted,
because this step requires the same number of target assays for all
strategies being compared.
\item[\cde{f}] Flanking-marker preselection is counted as single-marker (SM)
assays.  In advanced generations, markers already fixed for the recurrent
allele in the selected non-recurrent parent need not be assayed again.
\item[\cde{b}] Genome-wide background selection is counted as one
high-throughput (HT) assay per assayed individual, irrespective of marker
genotype.
\item[\cde{s}] Selection for a low number of donor segments is also counted
as one HT assay per assayed individual.
\item[\cde{r}] Recipient-locus preselection is treated as an HT assay.  In
\cde{tr}, \cde{trb}, and \cde{trfb}, one HT assay is counted for every plant
carrying the target genes.  In \cde{tfrb}, flanking selection precedes the
recipient-locus step and the flanking assays are counted as SM assays.
\end{description}

The optional \cde{success.factor} is the probability that a selected plant
survives and is fertile.  Values below one cause the number of successful
selected individuals to be drawn from a binomial distribution; at least one
individual is retained.

\subsection{\cde{mab.simulate()}}

\cde{mab.simulate()} defines one complete program.  Its principal inputs are
the simulation identifiers, number of repetitions, donor and recurrent
parents, linkage map, target/flanking/recipient loci, generation types,
population sizes, selection strategies, numbers selected, optional minimum
numbers preselected, optional separately evaluated chromosome regions,
missing-allele code, success factor, and the explicit result file.

A successful call invisibly returns the same named list that is saved to the
result file.  Depending on the requested analyses, the result contains
target-allele summaries, genome-wide and regional RPG summaries,
genome-wide and regional donor-segment counts, linkage drag for each target,
and SM and HT marker-analysis counts.

\subsection{\cde{mab.load.data()} and \cde{mab.tabulate()}}

\cde{mab.load.data()} reads one saved result.  The simulation name and run
remain part of the workflow identifiers, but the file itself is selected
explicitly by \cde{result.file}.

\cde{mab.tabulate()} accepts alternating simulation names and run identifiers
in \cde{simulation.names.runs} and one explicitly supplied result file for
each scenario.  It can return a basic parameter summary, an extended
parameter summary, mean and Q10 RPG values, target-allele summaries, regional
RPG, donor-segment counts, linkage drag, and SM/HT marker-analysis counts.
RPG can be reported as percent (the default) or as a proportion, and the
number of displayed decimal places is controlled by \cde{digits}.

The following complete example demonstrates the three functions.

% MABC example: high_core
<<high_core_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2000)
@
The first simulation example uses the shared directory setup and fixes
a seed.  High-level routines read maps from
\cde{st.input.dir} and save result objects in \cde{st.data.dir}.

<<high_core_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.simulate(
    simulation.name="core",
    simulation.run="x",
    repetitions=NREP,
    linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    target.loci="target",
    flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc"),
    population.size=c(1,100,100,100),
    sel.strategy=c("n","tfb","tfb","tfb"),
    result.file="core.RData"
)
@
A complete breeding program is described in one \cde{mab.simulate()} call:
one F1 generation followed by three backcrosses, target selection,
population size 100, and one selected plant per generation.

<<high_core_03,eval=FALSE>>=
loaded <- mab.load.data(
    simulation.name="core",
    simulation.run="x",
    result.file="core.RData"
)
@
\cde{mab.load.data()} retrieves the saved simulation object explicitly from its result file.  Loading and simulation are therefore separate operations.

<<high_core_04,eval=FALSE>>=
tab <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c("core","x"),
    ext.summary=TRUE,
    TargetAlleles=TRUE,
    RPG.gw.mean=TRUE,
    RPG.gw.Q10=TRUE,
    DonorSegments.gw=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    MarkerAnalyses=TRUE,
    result.files="core.RData"
)
@
\cde{mab.tabulate()} requests selected summaries from the same saved result.  Here the target-allele state, mean RPG, linkage drag, and marker-analysis counts are requested together.

<<high_core_05,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- list(
    result.components=names(loaded),
    selected.summary=tab
)
print(out)
@
The printed list demonstrates the two levels of access: the loaded object
is available for detailed inspection, whereas the tabulation provides the
usual compact MABC summaries.

\begin{Soutput}
