\subsection{\cde{mab.compare()}}

\cde{mab.compare()} can run up to nine scenarios, labeled A through I.
Scenario A is the complete base definition.  A later scenario is activated
by giving its \cde{simulation.name}; every other \cde{NULL} setting then
inherits the corresponding A setting.  Thus only the parameters being
changed need to be supplied.  \cde{result.files} contains one unique file
name for every active scenario in A--I order.

This inheritance mechanism is used repeatedly in the advanced examples
below to compare population sizes, selection strategies, parental
genotypes, and survival assumptions.

\subsection{Interpreting MABC results}

The main output quantities have direct breeding interpretations.

\begin{description}
\item[Target alleles.] \cde{TargetAlleles} summarizes the frequency of donor
target alleles among the selected plants over generations.
\item[Genome-wide RPG.] \cde{RPG.gw} summarizes recurrent-parent genome
content over the complete genome.  \cde{mab.tabulate()} can report its mean
and its 10th percentile (Q10).
\item[Regional RPG.] \cde{RPG.reg1}, \cde{RPG.reg2}, \ldots{} summarize RPG
within chromosome regions defined by \cde{reg.chr}, \cde{reg.begin}, and
\cde{reg.end}.  An \cde{reg.end} of zero means the chromosome end.
\item[Donor segments.] \cde{DonorSegments.gw} and the corresponding regional
objects count donor chromosome segments.  Segments are counted per
homologue, so the two homologues of a diploid chromosome are counted
separately.
\item[Linkage drag.] \cde{LinkageDrag1}, \cde{LinkageDrag2}, \ldots{} give
the donor chromosome segment attached to each target locus.  Target loci are
evaluated separately and linkage drag is averaged over the two homologues.
\item[Marker analyses.] \cde{SM} and \cde{HT} summarize the single-marker and
high-throughput marker analyses attributed to the selected strategy.
\end{description}

These quantities allow strategies to be compared simultaneously for genetic
outcome and marker-analysis effort.

\subsection{Planning comparisons}

MABC optimization is usually a comparison problem rather than a single
simulation problem.  Population size, selection stage, number and position
of target genes, density of background markers, starting genotype,
preselection intensity, and survival of selected plants can all affect RPG
recovery, linkage drag, and marker-data requirements.

The high-level interface is designed around this idea.  A useful analysis
typically defines one base scenario with \cde{mab.compare()}, changes only
the factors of interest in scenarios B through I, and then requests the same
columns from \cde{mab.tabulate()} for all scenarios.  The examples in the
next section illustrate these comparisons with explicitly named result files.

\section{Advanced high-level examples}

The following subsections present high-level MABC comparisons.  After the
shared setup in the high-level workflow, each example can be run separately:
it uses a fixed seed, creates all result files it needs, and prints the
summary shown in the blue box.  The number of repetitions is
\cde{NREP <- 10000}.

\subsection{Example 1: six backcross generations with target selection}

This example corresponds to the structure of Table 2 of Frisch et al.\
(1999).  An F1 is followed by six backcross generations and two selfing
generations.  Strategy \cde{t} selects for the target allele only; marker
data points for target assays are not counted.

% MABC example: ex01
<<ex01_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2101)
@
Example 1 initializes both R and the internal random-number generators after the shared setup.

<<ex01_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.simulate(
    simulation.name="fbm99-01",
    simulation.run="x",
    repetitions=NREP,
    linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    target.loci="target",
    flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc","bc","bc","bc","s","s"),
    population.size=c(1,100,100,100,100,100,100,100,100),
    sel.strategy=c("n","t","t","t","t","t","t","t","t"),
    result.file="fbm99-01.RData"
)
@
The program follows an F1 with six backcross generations and two selfing
generations.  Selection strategy \cde{t} retains the target allele but
performs no marker-assisted background selection.

<<ex01_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c("fbm99-01","x"),
    TargetAlleles=TRUE,
    RPG.gw.mean=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    result.files="fbm99-01.RData"
)
print(out)
@
The tabulation reports target-allele recovery, genome-wide RPG, linkage
drag, and marker analyses for the saved scenario.  These quantities form
the baseline for the more intensive strategies below.

