\subsection{Example 2: two-stage selection}

The second historical example corresponds to the RPG comparison in Table 3
of Frisch et al.\ (1999).  Strategy \cde{tb} first selects plants carrying
the target allele and then performs genome-wide background selection.  The
second stage is treated as an HT assay.

The section regenerates both the target-only and two-stage scenarios so that
it can be run independently.

% MABC example: ex02
<<ex02_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2102)
@
Example 2 uses a separate seed and result files so that it can be run without Example 1.

<<ex02_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.simulate(
    simulation.name="fbm99-01",
    simulation.run="x",
    repetitions=NREP,
    linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    target.loci="target",
    flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc","bc","bc","bc","s","s"),
    population.size=c(1,100,100,100,100,100,100,100,100),
    sel.strategy=c("n","t","t","t","t","t","t","t","t"),
    result.file="fbm99-01.RData"
)
@
The first call reproduces target-only selection over five backcross
generations.  Keeping this scenario separate provides a direct comparator
for two-stage selection.

<<ex02_03,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.simulate(
    simulation.name="fbm99-02",
    simulation.run="x",
    repetitions=NREP,
    linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    target.loci="target",
    flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc","bc","bc"),
    population.size=c(1,100,100,100,100,100),
    sel.strategy=c("n","tb","tb","tb","tb","tb"),
    result.file="fbm99-02.RData"
)
@
The second program changes only the backcross selection strategy to
\cde{tb}: target carriers are identified first and genome-wide background
selection is then applied among them.

<<ex02_04,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c(
        "fbm99-01","x",
        "fbm99-02","x"
    ),
    TargetAlleles=TRUE,
    RPG.gw.mean=TRUE,
    RPG.gw.Q10=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    result.files=c("fbm99-01.RData","fbm99-02.RData")
)
print(out)
@
Both result files are tabulated jointly.  The RPG and linkage-drag
summaries show the benefit obtained from background selection, while
marker-analysis counts quantify the extra genotyping effort.

