\subsection{Example 3: increasing population sizes and three-stage selection}

This example follows the principle illustrated in Table 4 of Frisch et al.\
(1999): population sizes increase from 33 to 100 to 166 across BC1--BC3 and
strategy \cde{tfb} combines target, flanking-marker and genome-wide
background selection.

% MABC example: ex03
<<ex03_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2103)
@
Example 3 uses its own seed and the shared temporary result directory.

<<ex03_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.simulate(
    simulation.name="fbm99-03",
    simulation.run="x",
    repetitions=NREP,
    linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    target.loci="target",
    flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc"),
    population.size=c(1,33,100,166),
    sel.strategy=c("n","tfb","tfb","tfb"),
    result.file="fbm99-03.RData"
)
@
Population sizes increase from 33 to 100 and 166 across the three backcross
generations.  Strategy \cde{tfb} combines target, flanking-marker, and
genome-wide background selection.

<<ex03_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c("fbm99-03","x"),
    RPG.gw.Q10=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    MarkerAnalyses=TRUE,
    result.files="fbm99-03.RData"
)
print(out)
@
The requested summaries emphasize the lower tail of RPG, linkage drag, and
marker analyses.  These are the quantities used to evaluate whether
increasing population size improves the chance of obtaining favorable
recombinants.

\begin{Soutput}
$par.summary
