\subsection{Example 4: comparing population sizes}

\cde{mab.compare()} is used to define six otherwise identical \cde{tb}
programs with population sizes 60, 80, 100, 125, 150 and 200 in every backcross
generation, covering the larger population sizes in Table 3 of Frisch et al.\
(1999).
Only the scenario-specific population sizes and names need to be supplied
for scenarios B through F.

% MABC example: ex04
<<ex04_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2104)
@
The comparison uses the shared setup and \cde{NREP}, with its own fixed seed.

<<ex04_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.compare(
    a.simulation.name="s01-060",
    a.simulation.run="x",
    a.repetitions=NREP,
    a.linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    a.target.loci="target",
    a.flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    a.gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc"),
    a.population.size=c(1,60,60,60),
    a.sel.strategy=c("n","tb","tb","tb"),
    b.simulation.name="s01-080",
    b.population.size=c(1,80,80,80),
    c.simulation.name="s01-100",
    c.population.size=c(1,100,100,100),
    d.simulation.name="s01-125",
    d.population.size=c(1,125,125,125),
    e.simulation.name="s01-150",
    e.population.size=c(1,150,150,150),
    f.simulation.name="s01-200",
    f.population.size=c(1,200,200,200),
    result.files=c(
        "s01-060.RData",
        "s01-080.RData",
        "s01-100.RData",
        "s01-125.RData",
        "s01-150.RData",
        "s01-200.RData"
    )
)
@
\cde{mab.compare()} defines the 60-plant program as scenario A.  Scenarios B through F inherit every unspecified setting and replace only their names and population sizes, giving directly comparable programs of 80, 100, 125, 150, and 200 plants.

<<ex04_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c(
        "s01-060","x",
        "s01-080","x",
        "s01-100","x",
        "s01-125","x",
        "s01-150","x",
        "s01-200","x"
    ),
    RPG.gw.Q10=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    MarkerAnalyses=TRUE,
    result.files=c(
        "s01-060.RData",
        "s01-080.RData",
        "s01-100.RData",
        "s01-125.RData",
        "s01-150.RData",
        "s01-200.RData"
    )
)
print(out)
@
The six result files are tabulated together for the 10\% RPG quantile,
linkage drag, and marker analyses.  Joint tabulation is the convenient way
to compare alternative program designs.

