\subsection{Example 5: simultaneous introgression of two genes}

This example corresponds to the structure of Table 5 of Frisch and
Melchinger (2001).  Two target genes are introgressed simultaneously.  Four
flanking markers define the target regions, and the population increases
from 60 to 120 to 180 across BC1--BC3.  Arbitrary numbers of target genes
and flanking markers can be defined in the same way.
The example ends at BC3; Table 5 additionally includes a final selfing
generation and reports BC3-S1, so its final RPG and marker-analysis
values are not directly comparable to the BC3 values here.

% MABC example: ex05
<<ex05_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2105)
@
The two-gene introgression scenario uses the shared setup and its own seed.

<<ex05_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.simulate(
    simulation.name="fm01u-01",
    simulation.run="x",
    repetitions=NREP,
    linkage.map="ex-mab2.map",
    target.loci=c("target1","target2"),
    flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27","umc111","bnl1407"),
    gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc"),
    population.size=c(1,60,120,180),
    sel.strategy=c("n","tfb","tfb","tfb"),
    result.file="fm01u-01.RData"
)
@
Two target loci and four flanking markers are supplied.  Three backcross
generations use increasing population sizes and \cde{tfb} selection to
recover both target alleles while controlling linkage drag and background
recovery.

<<ex05_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c("fm01u-01","x"),
    TargetAlleles=TRUE,
    RPG.gw.mean=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    MarkerAnalyses=TRUE,
    result.files="fm01u-01.RData"
)
print(out)
@
The result table requests target-allele recovery, RPG, linkage drag, and
marker analyses.  The same high-level interface therefore extends from one
target locus to multiple target loci without changing the simulation
workflow.

\begin{Soutput}
