\subsection{Example 6: selection strategy and linkage drag}

Three otherwise identical programs compare target-only selection
(\cde{t}), target plus background selection (\cde{tb}), and target plus
flanking-marker plus background selection (\cde{tfb}).  The comparison
shows how selection strategy changes RPG, linkage drag, and marker-analysis
requirements.

% MABC example: ex06
<<ex06_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2106)
@
Example 6 starts from the same simulation environment for all
strategies in the comparison.

<<ex06_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.compare(
    a.simulation.name="ld-t",
    a.simulation.run="x",
    a.repetitions=NREP,
    a.linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    a.target.loci="target",
    a.flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    a.gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc"),
    a.population.size=c(1,100,100,100),
    a.sel.strategy=c("n","t","t","t"),
    a.no.selected=c(1,1,1,1),
    b.simulation.name="ld-tb",
    b.sel.strategy=c("n","tb","tb","tb"),
    c.simulation.name="ld-tfb",
    c.sel.strategy=c("n","tfb","tfb","tfb"),
    result.files=c("ld-t.RData","ld-tb.RData","ld-tfb.RData")
)
@
The base scenario uses target-only selection.  Two inherited scenarios
replace that strategy by \cde{tb} and \cde{tfb}, so the effect of adding
background and flanking-marker selection can be examined without changing
other design parameters.

<<ex06_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c(
        "ld-t","x",
        "ld-tb","x",
        "ld-tfb","x"
    ),
    RPG.gw.Q10=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    MarkerAnalyses=TRUE,
    result.files=c("ld-t.RData","ld-tb.RData","ld-tfb.RData")
)
print(out)
@
The joint table focuses on RPG, linkage drag, and marker analyses.  This
exposes the central trade-off between faster recurrent-genome recovery,
reduced donor segment around the target, and genotyping effort.

\begin{Soutput}
