\subsection{Example 8: observed marker data for donor and recurrent parent}

Instead of assuming two fully polymorphic homozygous lines, observed SNP
marker files define both parents.  If one allele is supplied at a marker,
the parent is treated as homozygous there.  Only markers with known map
positions and usable marker data in both parents are retained.  Missing
alleles are coded by \cde{"9"} in this example.

The map \cde{ex-mab4.map} contains 320 marker rows and the parental files
contain the corresponding observed alleles.  The target is
\cde{s7e2190xxx}, flanked by \cde{s7e5163xxx} and
\cde{s7e4815xxx}.

% MABC example: ex08
<<ex08_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2108)
@
This example uses observed parental marker files rather than idealized
homozygous allele codes, while using the shared setup and its own
fixed seed.

<<ex08_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.compare(
    a.simulation.name="marker-01",
    a.simulation.run="x",
    a.repetitions=NREP,
    a.donor.parent="ex-mabp1_m.mda",
    a.recurrent.parent="ex-mabp2_m.mda",
    a.linkage.map="ex-mab4.map",
    a.target.loci="s7e2190xxx",
    a.flanking.loci=c("s7e5163xxx","s7e4815xxx"),
    a.gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc"),
    a.population.size=c(1,25,25),
    a.sel.strategy=c("n","t","t"),
    a.missing.allele="9",
    b.simulation.name="marker-02",
    b.population.size=c(1,50,50),
    b.sel.strategy=c("n","tb","tb"),
    result.files=c("marker-01.RData","marker-02.RData")
)
@
The donor and recurrent-parent files, observed linkage map, target locus,
flanking loci, and missing-allele symbol are supplied to
\cde{mab.compare()}.  A second scenario doubles the backcross population
size and adds background selection.

<<ex08_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c(
        "marker-01","x",
        "marker-02","x"
    ),
    RPG.gw.Q10=TRUE,
    DonorSegments.gw=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    result.files=c("marker-01.RData","marker-02.RData")
)
print(out)
@
Joint tabulation compares RPG, donor-segment number, and linkage drag.
These summaries demonstrate that the high-level interface can work from
actual parental marker genotypes as well as simulated homogeneous parents.

