\subsection{Example 9: different BC1 plants as donor parent}

The non-recurrent starting parent need not be a homozygous line.  Here six
different BC1 plants, each represented by a two-allele marker file, are
backcrossed for two additional generations.  The selected BC3 descendants
are compared for RPG, linkage drag, and number of donor segments.

% MABC example: ex09
<<ex09_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2109)
@
Each observed BC1 donor comparison uses the shared setup, its own seed,
and the supplied individual marker files.

<<ex09_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.compare(
    a.simulation.name="bc1i1tfb",
    a.simulation.run="x",
    a.repetitions=NREP,
    a.donor.parent="ex-mabi1.mda",
    a.recurrent.parent="ex-mabp2.mda",
    a.linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    a.target.loci="target",
    a.flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    a.gen.type=c("bc","bc"),
    a.population.size=c(100,100),
    a.sel.strategy=c("tfb","tfb"),
    b.simulation.name="bc1i2tfb",
    b.donor.parent="ex-mabi2.mda",
    c.simulation.name="bc1i3tfb",
    c.donor.parent="ex-mabi3.mda",
    d.simulation.name="bc1i4tfb",
    d.donor.parent="ex-mabi4.mda",
    e.simulation.name="bc1i5tfb",
    e.donor.parent="ex-mabi5.mda",
    f.simulation.name="bc1i6tfb",
    f.donor.parent="ex-mabi6.mda",
    result.files=paste0("bc1i",1:6,"tfb.RData")
)
@
Scenario A defines the common recurrent parent, map, target, flanking
markers, and two subsequent backcrosses.  Scenarios B--F inherit these
settings and differ only in the BC1 donor genotype file.

<<ex09_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=as.vector(rbind(
        paste0("bc1i",1:6,"tfb"),
        rep("x",6)
    )),
    RPG.gw.Q10=TRUE,
    DonorSegments.gw=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    result.files=paste0("bc1i",1:6,"tfb.RData")
)
print(out)
@
The six resulting programs are compared for RPG, genome-wide donor-segment
number, and linkage drag.  This permits candidate BC1 plants to be
evaluated by their expected consequences in later backcross generations.

\begin{Soutput}
