\subsection{Example 9a: analysis of input individuals without further simulation}

If \cde{gen.type}, \cde{population.size}, and \cde{sel.strategy} are omitted,
the supplied parental/input individuals themselves can be analyzed.  This
allows the six BC1 plants from the preceding example to be compared without
simulating additional generations.

% MABC example: ex09a
<<ex09a_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2110)
@
The analysis-only version uses the same observed BC1 donor files but does
not define future generations.

<<ex09a_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.compare(
    a.simulation.name="bc1i1",
    a.simulation.run="x",
    a.donor.parent="ex-mabi1.mda",
    a.recurrent.parent="ex-mabp2.mda",
    a.linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    a.target.loci="target",
    a.flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    b.simulation.name="bc1i2",
    b.donor.parent="ex-mabi2.mda",
    c.simulation.name="bc1i3",
    c.donor.parent="ex-mabi3.mda",
    d.simulation.name="bc1i4",
    d.donor.parent="ex-mabi4.mda",
    e.simulation.name="bc1i5",
    e.donor.parent="ex-mabi5.mda",
    f.simulation.name="bc1i6",
    f.donor.parent="ex-mabi6.mda",
    result.files=paste0("bc1i",1:6,".RData")
)
@
\cde{mab.compare()} therefore imports and evaluates the six input genotypes without simulating additional progeny.  The inherited scenarios again differ only in the donor-parent file.

<<ex09a_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=as.vector(rbind(
        paste0("bc1i",1:6),
        rep("x",6)
    )),
    RPG.gw.Q10=TRUE,
    DonorSegments.gw=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    result.files=paste0("bc1i",1:6,".RData")
)
print(out)
@
The tabulation compares the existing individuals for RPG, donor segments,
and linkage drag.  This is useful when selection decisions must be made
from already genotyped backcross plants.

