\subsection{Example 11: analysis of chromosome regions}

RPG and donor segments can be evaluated for user-defined regions.  The
example defines chromosome 1, chromosome 5, and the interval 0--60 cM on
chromosome 5.  A zero value for \cde{reg.end} means the complete chromosome.

% MABC example: ex11
<<ex11_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2112)
@
This regional-analysis example uses a target-only program with large
backcross populations and its own simulation seed.

<<ex11_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.simulate(
    simulation.name="regions-1",
    simulation.run="x",
    repetitions=NREP,
    linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    target.loci="target",
    flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc"),
    population.size=c(1,500,500,500),
    sel.strategy=c("n","t","t","t"),
    no.selected=c(1,1,1,1),
    reg.chr=c(1,5,5),
    reg.begin=c(0,0,0),
    reg.end=c(0,0,60),
    result.file="regions-1.RData"
)
@
Three regions are requested: complete chromosome 1, complete target
chromosome 5, and the 0--60 cM interval on chromosome 5.  Region
definitions are stored with the simulation and evaluated along with
genome-wide quantities.

<<ex11_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c("regions-1","x"),
    ext.summary=TRUE,
    RPG.gw.mean=TRUE,
    RPG.reg.mean=TRUE,
    DonorSegments.gw=TRUE,
    DonorSegments.reg=TRUE,
    result.files="regions-1.RData"
)
print(out)
@
The tabulation asks for mean RPG and donor-segment counts both genome-wide
and by region.  Regional output is useful when recovery near the target or
in another biologically important chromosome segment matters separately
from whole-genome recovery.

