\subsection{Example 12: preselection for recurrent-parent alleles}

\cde{recipient.loci} defines markers at which recurrent-parent alleles must
be retained.  The historical example places these loci on chromosome 2 and
therefore also evaluates RPG for chromosome 2 separately.

The recipient-locus comparison uses strategies \cde{tr}, \cde{trb},
\cde{trfb}, and \cde{tfrb}.  In \cde{tr} and \cde{trb}, recipient-locus
selection follows target selection.  \cde{trfb} adds flanking and background
selection after the recipient-locus step.  \cde{tfrb} performs flanking
selection before recipient-locus and background selection.  The comparison
includes these strategies together with \cde{t}, \cde{tf}, and
\cde{tfb}.  To run the simulations as independent units, the
example runs each strategy with its own fixed seed and tabulates the
seven result files jointly.

% MABC example: ex12
<<ex12_01,eval=FALSE>>=
recipient <- c(
    "bnl845","umc53","umc6","umc34","umc131",
    "umc98a","umc4","umc10","bnl844b","umc36"
)
@
The recipient-locus comparison first defines the common recipient-locus
panel after the shared setup.  These markers represent
recurrent-parent alleles that should be retained.

<<ex12_02,eval=FALSE>>=
run.recipient <- function(name,strategy,seed) {
    rng.init(seed=seed)
    mab.simulate(
        simulation.name=name,
        simulation.run="x",
        repetitions=NREP,
        linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
        target.loci="target",
        flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
        recipient.loci=recipient,
        gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc"),
        population.size=c(1,1000,1000,1000),
        sel.strategy=c("n",strategy,strategy,strategy),
        no.selected=c(1,1,1,1),
        reg.chr=c(1,2,5,5),
        reg.begin=c(0,0,0,0),
        reg.end=c(0,0,0,60),
        result.file=paste0(name,".RData")
    )
}
@
The helper function packages the common breeding design.  Only the scenario
name, selection strategy, and seed vary; every strategy is therefore
simulated as a standalone program with the same populations,
regions, target, flanking markers, and recipient loci.

<<ex12_03,eval=FALSE>>=
run.recipient("ss-t",   "t",   21131)
run.recipient("ss-tf",  "tf",  21132)
run.recipient("ss-tr",  "tr",  21133)
run.recipient("ss-tfb", "tfb", 21134)
run.recipient("ss-trb", "trb", 21135)
run.recipient("ss-trfb","trfb",21136)
run.recipient("ss-tfrb","tfrb",21137)
@
Seven calls cover \cde{t}, \cde{tf}, \cde{tr}, \cde{tfb}, \cde{trb},
\cde{trfb}, and \cde{tfrb}.  Separate calls initialize both R and the internal random-number generators for each scenario
while preserving a joint comparison.

<<ex12_04,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c(
        "ss-t","x",
        "ss-tf","x",
        "ss-tr","x",
        "ss-tfb","x",
        "ss-trb","x",
        "ss-trfb","x",
        "ss-tfrb","x"
    ),
    ext.summary=TRUE,
    RPG.gw.mean=TRUE,
    RPG.reg.mean=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    MarkerAnalyses=TRUE,
    result.files=c(
        "ss-t.RData","ss-tf.RData","ss-tr.RData",
        "ss-tfb.RData","ss-trb.RData",
        "ss-trfb.RData","ss-tfrb.RData"
    )
)
print(out)
@
The seven saved result files are tabulated together.  Extended parameter
summaries, genome-wide and regional RPG, linkage drag, and marker analyses
reveal how the ordering and inclusion of recipient-, flanking-, and
background-selection stages change the program.

\begin{Soutput}
$ext.summary
