\subsection{Example 14: recoding parental input files}

With \cde{recode.infiles=TRUE}, parental marker data can be converted to the
internal donor/recurrent-parent coding and written to an explicitly supplied
\cde{recode.file}.  In the recoded representation the recurrent-parent
allele is coded as 8.  Both
\cde{result.file} and \cde{recode.file} must be supplied explicitly.

% MABC example: ex14
<<ex14_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2115)
@
The recoding example uses observed parental marker data and a separate seed
but does not need to simulate a breeding program.

<<ex14_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.simulate(
    simulation.name="rec-01",
    simulation.run="x",
    donor.parent="ex-mabp1_m.mda",
    recurrent.parent="ex-mabp2_m.mda",
    linkage.map="ex-mab4.map",
    result.file="rec-01.RData",
    recode.infiles=TRUE,
    recode.file="recoded-parents.mda"
)
@
With \cde{recode.infiles=TRUE}, \cde{mab.simulate()} creates recoded
parental input in which alleles are expressed relative to donor and
recurrent parents.  The linkage map and missing-allele code determine which
marker records can be used.

<<ex14_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- list(
    result=names(mab.load.data(
        simulation.name="rec-01",
        simulation.run="x",
        result.file="rec-01.RData"
    )),
    recoded.file=head(
        readLines(file.path(st.output.dir,"recoded-parents.mda")),
        12
    )
)
print(out)
@
The example then locates the recoded output and prints its first
lines.  This makes the generated file format visible without filling the
vignette with the complete recoded marker matrix.

\begin{Soutput}
$result
 [1] "simulation.name"  "simulation.run"   "repetitions"     
 [4] "recurrent.parent" "donor.parent"     "linkage.map"     
 [7] "sel.strategy"     "no.selected"      "success.factor"  
[10] "RPG.gw"           "DonorSegments.gw"

$recoded.file
 [1] "01E0103DH03 01E0109DH02" "s1e0038xxx.1  1 0"      
 [3] "s1e0038xxx.8  0 1"       "s1e1093xxx.1  0 0"      
 [5] "s1e1093xxx.8  1 1"       "s1e2309xxx.1  0 0"      
 [7] "s1e2309xxx.8  1 1"       "s1e2406xxx.1  0 0"      
 [9] "s1e2406xxx.8  1 1"       "s1e2486xxx.1  0 0"      
[11] "s1e2486xxx.8  1 1"       "s1e2522xxx.1  0 0"      

\end{Soutput}

\subsection{Example 15: minimum number of preselected individuals}

For strategies containing the flanking-marker step \cde{f}, the default is
to retain all individuals having the maximum number of favorable flanking
markers.  This can produce a very small preselected set.  For example, if
only two plants show recombination on both sides of a target locus, only
those two would proceed to the HT assay in a \cde{tfb} strategy.

\cde{no.preselected} imposes a minimum size on that intermediate set.  If
five plants are requested and only two attain the best flanking-marker
class, three additional plants from the next-best class are added.  The
example compares the default behavior with a minimum of five preselected
plants in each backcross generation.

% MABC example: ex15
<<ex15_01,eval=FALSE>>=
rng.init(seed=2116)
@
The final example again defines all common settings locally so the
comparison can be regenerated by itself.

<<ex15_02,eval=FALSE>>=
mab.compare(
    a.simulation.name="sel-50-0a-01",
    a.simulation.run="x",
    a.repetitions=NREP,
    a.linkage.map="ex-mab1.map",
    a.target.loci="target",
    a.flanking.loci=c("umc90","umc27"),
    a.gen.type=c("f1","bc","bc","bc"),
    a.population.size=c(1,50,50,50),
    a.sel.strategy=c("n","tfb","tfb","tfb"),
    b.simulation.name="sel-50-05-01",
    b.no.preselected=c(1,5,5,5),
    result.files=c("sel-50-0a-01.RData","sel-50-05-01.RData")
)
@
Both scenarios use \cde{tfb} with population size 50.  Scenario B sets
\cde{no.preselected=5}, forcing at least five plants to pass the
flanking-marker preselection even when fewer individuals carry the maximum
number of favorable flanking alleles.

<<ex15_03,eval=FALSE>>=
out <- mab.tabulate(
    simulation.names.runs=c(
        "sel-50-0a-01","x",
        "sel-50-05-01","x"
    ),
    ext.summary=TRUE,
    RPG.gw.Q10=TRUE,
    LinkageDrag=TRUE,
    MarkerAnalyses=TRUE,
    result.files=c("sel-50-0a-01.RData","sel-50-05-01.RData")
)
print(out)
@
The two programs are compared for the lower RPG quantile, linkage drag, and
marker analyses.  The example shows explicitly how relaxing the flanking
preselection cutoff changes both selection opportunity and genotyping
effort.

\begin{Soutput}
